2026-06-30 15:02:10,612 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-8a849501-3a7d-42f5-b042-1952f751d282', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nF1801_sailerc_031.raw\nF1801_sailerc_032.raw\nF1801_sailerc_033.raw\nF1801_sailerc_034.raw\nF1903_sailerc_185.raw\nF1903_sailerc_186.raw\nF1903_sailerc_187.raw\nF1903_sailerc_188.raw\nF1903_sailerc_189.raw\nF1903_sailerc_190.raw\nF1903_sailerc_193.raw\nF1903_sailerc_194.raw\nF1903_sailerc_195.raw\nF1903_sailerc_196.raw\nF1903_sailerc_197.raw\nF1903_sailerc_198.raw\nSumoE1_1A_F1806_sailerc_256.raw\nSumoE1_1A_F1806_sailerc_257.raw\nSumoE1_1A_F1806_sailerc_258.raw\nSumoE1_1A_F1806_sailerc_259.raw\nSumoE1_1B_F1806_sailerc_262.raw\nSumoE1_1B_F1806_sailerc_263.raw\nSumoE1_1B_F1806_sailerc_264.raw\nSumoE1_1B_F1806_sailerc_265.raw\nSumoE1_1C_F1806_sailerc_268.raw\nSumoE1_1C_F1806_sailerc_269.raw\nSumoE1_1C_F1806_sailerc_270.raw\nSumoE1_1C_F1806_sailerc_271.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2A_F1806_sailerc_240.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2A_F1806_sailerc_275.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2A_F1806_sailerc_276.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2A_F1806_sailerc_277.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2B_F1806_sailerc_243.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2B_F1806_sailerc_244.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2B_F1806_sailerc_245.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2B_F1806_sailerc_246.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2C_F1806_sailerc_249.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2C_F1806_sailerc_250.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2C_F1806_sailerc_251.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_2C_F1806_sailerc_252.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3A_F1806_sailerc_214.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3A_F1806_sailerc_215.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3A_F1806_sailerc_216.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3A_F1806_sailerc_217.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3B_F1806_sailerc_220.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3B_F1806_sailerc_221.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3B_F1806_sailerc_286.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3B_F1806_sailerc_287.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3C_F1806_sailerc_281.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3C_F1806_sailerc_282.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3C_F1806_sailerc_283.raw\nSumoE1_Sumo_Fat10_3C_F1806_sailerc_284.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nThe ubiquitin-like modifier FAT10 interferes with SUMO activation\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe covalent attachment of the cytokine-inducible ubiquitin-like modifier HLA-F adjacent transcript 10 (FAT10) to hundreds of substrate proteins leads to their rapid degradation by the 26\u2009S proteasome independently of ubiquitylation. Here, we identify another function of FAT10, showing that it interferes with the activation of SUMO1/2/3 in vitro and down-regulates SUMO conjugation and the SUMO-dependent formation of promyelocytic leukemia protein (PML) bodies in cells. Mechanistically, we show that FAT10 directly binds to and impedes the activity of the heterodimeric SUMO E1 activating enzyme AOS1/UBA2 by competing very efficiently with SUMO for activation and thioester formation. Nevertheless, activation of FAT10 by AOS1/UBA2 does not lead to covalent conjugation of FAT10 with substrate proteins which relies on its cognate E1 enzyme UBA6. Hence, we report that one ubiquitin-like modifier (FAT10) inhibits the conjugation and function of another ubiquitin-like modifier (SUMO) by impairing its activation.\nFAT10 is an ubiquitin-like modifier that targets proteins to proteasomal degradation. Here, the authors show that FAT10 also regulates SUMO activation in vitro and in cells, providing evidence for functional crosstalk between two ubiquitin-like modifiers.\n\n[METHODS]\nMethods\nPlasmids\nPlasmids used for transient expression of JunB variants in HEK293 cells were pCMV-myc-DDK-JunB (JunB-FLAG) (Origene), pcDNA3-JunB-HA, pcDNA3-JunB-HA-K237R, pcDNA3-JunB-HA-K267R, pcDNA3-JunB-HA-K301R, and pcDNA3-JunB-HA-K3R (, kindly provided by Dr. Marc Piechaczyk, Montpellier). For expression of FAT10 variants, the following plasmids were used: pcDNA3.1-HA-FAT10, pcDNA3.1-HA-FAT10-AV, and pcDNA-His-3xFLAG-FAT10 (FLAG-FAT10). pcDNA3.1-His/-A (Invitrogen) was used to balance plasmid amounts. For the expression of AOS1/UBA2 in HEK293 cells, pCRUZ-HA-AOS1 and pcDNA3-HA-UBA2 were used. For generation of pcDNA3-HA-UBA2, UBA2 was amplified by PCR from HEK293T cDNA and cloned with Asp718 and XhoI into pcDNA3.1-HA-UBE1L2. Plasmids for recombinant protein expression in E. coli were pET11d-UBA2 and pET28α-His-AOS1 (both previously described). pET11a-UBA2-C173A was generated by site-directed mutagenesis of pET11d-UBA2 with primers UBA2 SDM C173A fwd 5′-GAAGGTGTGTTACGAATTGTGGCGCCAGGAAAGGTTCTCTGGG-3′ and UBA2 SDM C173A rev 5′-CCCAGAGAACCTTTCCTGGCGCCACAATTCGTAACACACCTTC-3′. Recombinant FAT10 or the cysteine-free FAT10 variant FAT10(C0) were expressed from pSUMO-FAT10 or pSUMO-FAT10(C0), respectively. A FAT10 variant with the C-terminal diglycine motif changed from GG to AV was expressed from pSUMO FAT10-AV. For recombinant expression of a FAT10 variant missing the C-terminal diglycine motif, pSUMO-FAT10ΔGG was generated with primers 5′-BsaI-FAT10 5′-CCAGTGGGTCTCAGGTGGTGCTCCCAATGCTTCCTGCCTCTGTGTGC-3′ and 3′-XhoI-dGG-STOP-FAT10-SYC 5′-CCGCTCGAGTTAAATACAATAAGATGCCAGGAAGAGTAAG-3′. To generate the bacterial expression construct pET24α-JunB-HA-6His, JunB was amplified from pCMV6-JunB-myc-DDK (Origene) with primers 5′-BamHI-hJunB 5′-CGCGGATCCATGTGCACTAAAATGGAACAGCCC-3′ and 3′-XhoI-hJunB 5′-CCGCTCGAGGAAGGCGTGTCCCTTGACC-3′ and inserted via restrictions sites BamHI and XhoI into pET24α. The HA-Tag was synthesized with XhoI restriction sites 5′-TCGAGTACCCCTACGACGTGCCCGACTACGCCC-3′ (Microsynth, Balgach, Switzerland) and inserted 3`between JunB and the 6His-tag to create pET24α-JunB-HA-6His.\nRecombinant proteins and in vitro assays\nGST and GST-FAT10 were purified as follows: plasmids pGEX4T-3 or pGEX4T-3-FAT10 were transformed into E. coli BL21(DE3) and expression of GST or GST-FAT10 was induced with 0.1\u2009mM Isopropyl-β-D-thiogalactoside (IPTG) for 5\u2009h at 20\u2009°C. Cells were harvested, dissolved in 5\u2009ml PBS/g bacterial pellet, lysed (Cell Disruptor, Constant Systems) and PMSF was added to a final concentration of 1\u2009mM. The cell suspension was cleared by centrifugation at 4\u2009°C for 30\u2009min at 32,000\u2009×\u2009g, filtered through a 0.45\u2009µm filter and incubated for 2\u2009hours at 8\u2009°C with 5\u2009ml glutathione agarose (Sigma), pre-equilibrated in PBS. The glutathione agarose was washed two times with PBS and once with 50\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.0. Elution of GST or GST-FAT10 was performed by incubating the glutathione agarose with 2\u2009ml 50\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.0, supplemented with 5\u2009mM glutathione. Eluted protein lysates were subsequently desalted using PD-10 columns (GE Healthcare), as described by the manufacturer. Recombinant SUMO E2 (UBC9), MDYKDDDDK-tagged UBA6 (FLAG-UBA6), 6His-UBE1L (His-UBE1L), SUMO-1, 6His-ISG15 (His-ISG15), linear di-ubiquitin (linear di-Ub) and 6His-ubiquitin (His-Ub) were purchased from Enzo Lifesciences (Lausen, Switzerland). 6His-SUMO-2 was purchased from BostonBiochem. Expression plasmids pSUMO-HA-UBE1; pSUMO-FAT10; pSUMO-FAT10(C0); pSUMO-FAT10-AV and pSUMO-FAT10ΔGG were used to purify HA-UBE1 and the respective untagged FAT10 variants. Briefly, expression was performed in E. coli BL21(DE3) overnight at 21\u2009°C upon induction of protein expression with 0.4\u2009mM IPTG. Cells were harvested and resuspended in 5\u2009ml of binding buffer (20\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 150\u2009mM NaCl, 20\u2009mM imidazole, 1\u2009mM TCEP and 1 tablet/100\u2009ml cOmpleteTM, Mini, EDTA-free Protase Inhibitor Cocktail Tablet (Roche, Rotkreuz, Switzerland))/g bacterial pellet. Cells were lysed with at least two cycles at 2,5 kbar in a cell disrupter (Constant Cell Disrupter TS, Constant Systems Ltd.). Lysates were cleared by two filtration steps through filters with 1.2\u2009μm and 0.45\u2009μm particle pores, respectively, and loaded onto a pre-equilibrated 5\u2009ml HisTrap FF column (GE Healthcare) using AektaExplorer (GE Healthcare) driven by the UNICORN software (GE Healthcare). Unspecifically bound proteins were removed by washing with ten column volumes of binding buffer and 5 column volumes of binding buffer containing 5% of elution buffer (20\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 150\u2009mM NaCl, 0,5\u2009M imidazole, 1\u2009mM TCEP). Subsequent elution was performed with five column volumes of elution buffer. Imidazole was removed by size exclusion chromatography (HiPrep 26/10 desalting column, GE Healthcare) preequilibrated in binding buffer. Elution was performed with binding buffer until baseline. The 6xHis-SUMO-tag was removed by incubating the eluted proteins with 4\u2009μg ULP-1–6xHis/mg protein overnight at 4\u2009°C on a roller and subsequent loading onto a 5\u2009ml HisTrap FF column equilibrated in binding buffer. Elution of untagged recombinant HA-UBE1 or FAT10 variants was achieved by collecting the flow through while loading the sample onto the column. A final polishing step was performed by cation exchange chromatography using a HiScreen Capto SP ImpRes (GE Healthcare) preequilibrated in binding buffer (20\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 150\u2009mM NaCl). Elution of recombinant proteins was achieved by a gradient elution (0–50%) with 20 column volumes of elution buffer (20\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 2\u2009M NaCl). A final buffer exchange with a HiPrep 26/10 column was performed and recombinant proteins were stored at −80\u2009°C in storage buffer (20\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 150\u2009mM NaCl, 5% glycerol). Recombinant AOS1/UBA2 was purified as described. In short, pET11d-UBA2 and pET28α-His-AOS1 were transformed into E. coli BL21(DE3) and simultaneous expression of AOS1 and UBA2 was induced with 1\u2009mM IPTG for six hours at 25\u2009°C. Cells were harvested and resuspended in 5\u2009ml lysis buffer (20\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 300\u2009mM NaCl, 10\u2009mM imidazole, 2\u2009mM TCEP and 1 tablet/100\u2009ml cOmpleteTM, Mini, EDTA-free Protase Inhibitor Cocktail Tablet (Roche, Rotkreuz, Switzerland))/g bacterial pellet. Upon mechanical lysis as described above, PMSF was added to a final concentration of 1\u2009mM. Lysates were cleared by centrifugation (30,000\u2009×\u2009g, 4\u2009°C, 30\u2009min), filtered through a 0.45\u2009μm filter and incubated with 6\u2009ml Ni2+ beads (Takara), pre-equilibrated in binding buffer (20\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 300\u2009mM NaCl, 10\u2009mM imidazole, 1\u2009mM TCEP) for 1\u2009hour at 8\u2009°C with rolling. Beads were subsequently loaded onto a 1/10 EconoColumn (BioRad) and washed with wash buffer (20\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 300\u2009mM NaCl, 20\u2009mM imidazole, 1\u2009mM TCEP) until baseline. Elution of AOS1/UBA2 was performed with three column volumes of elution buffer (20\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 300\u2009mM NaCl, 250\u2009mM imidazole, 1\u2009mM TCEP). The eluate was further subjected to size exclusion chromatography (HiLoad 16/60 Superdex 200 prep grade) and anion exchange chromatography (Resource Q 1\u2009ml), desalted using PD10 desalting columns (GE Healthcare), and stored at −80\u2009°C in 40\u2009mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 100\u2009mM NaCl, 20\u2009mM MgCl2 and 1\u2009mM TCEP. Recombinant HA-SUMO-1 was purified from E. coli BL21(DE3) transformed with pET23a-HA-SUMO-1. Protein expression was performed upon addition of 0.4\u2009mM IPTG for 20\u2009h at 21\u2009°C. Cells were harvested, resuspended in 5\u2009ml of binding buffer (50\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 25\u2009mM NaCl, 1\u2009mM TCEP and one tablet / 100\u2009ml cOmpleteTM, Mini, EDTA-free Protease Inhibitor Cocktail Tablet (Roche, Rotkreuz, Switzerland))/ / g bacterial pellet and lysed by mechanical disruption as described above. PMSF was added to a final concentration of 1\u2009mM before the lysate was cleared by centrifugation and filtered using a 0.45\u2009µm filter. As a first purification step by anion exchange chromatography, the lysate was incubated with 4\u2009ml Q sepharose FF-beads (GE Healthcare), washed with five column volumes of binding buffer, five column volumes of wash buffer (50\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.8, 25\u2009mM NaCl, 1\u2009mM TCEP) and eluted with elution buffer (50\u2009mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, 500\u2009mM NaCl, 1\u2009mM TCEP). Upon a subsequent size exclusion chromatography (HiLoad 16/60 Superdex 75 prep grade), HA-SUMO-1 was stored at −80\u2009°C in 20\u2009mM HEPES pH 7.3, 110\u2009mM KOAc, 2\u2009mM Mg(OAC)2, 150\u2009mM NaCl, 5% glycerol and 1\u2009m TCEP. JunB-HA-His was purified from E. coli BL21(DE3) transformed with pET24α-JunB-HA-6His. Upon induction of protein expression with 0.4\u2009mM IPTG, cells were grown for 5\u2009hours at 30\u2009°C, harvested and dissolved in 5\u2009ml\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
